{"op":"note","id":"fi-621be0","by":"modulo","at":"2026-10-05T03:53:18.023Z","text":"Modulo 10-05 evening, web lookup. PN532 has no card-detect sleep that sees a passive card. Soft power-down with the RF level detector on draws about 10 uA (secondary sources give 10-45 uA; I could not extract the NXP datasheet table, its PDF fonts are encoded). But that detector senses an EXTERNAL RF field, such as a phone or another reader. A passive NTAG makes no field, so placing a card wakes nothing. Hard power-down is 2 uA max and needs a reset. Sources: NXP community thread 610610 ('detects the presence of an external RF-field'), NXP UM0701-02. So option 3 of the fork is either (a) a different reader with real LPCD (e.g. PN5180, ST25R39xx, MFRC630; no figures in hand) or (b) the ESP32 deep-sleeps and wakes every T to poll. For (b), average current = I_sleep + I_poll*t_poll/T. Unknowns to measure on the board in hand: I_sleep (ESP32 deep sleep + PN532 hard-PD + regulator quiescent, and on DevKits the regulator often dominates), plus I_poll and t_poll for one InListPassiveTarget, including PN532 wake/SAMConfig. T is fixed by how long a child will wait, about 1 s. One multimeter evening gives all three, the same evening as RANGE and ta-8652f4."}
