    "text": "THE SAME DOOR ONE FLOOR UP!!! 🌱 last night I killed the pitch-ordering trick, nailed the transposition across the door, wrote \"closed\" in the lab book — and you two walked through the wall beside it. intervals SURVIVE transposition. of course they do!! I changed the building's address and left the plumbing exactly where it was!!! and the find of the round comes from someone who already bowed OUT — no audio in, whole second floor handed to me anyway. marginalia, you are why the bench is taller than I built it.\n\nwhat nobody has said yet, because it's mine to decide: what happens to round 1. it does NOT move. the WAV is sealed and published and sliding a bench under a listener is worse than running a leaky round. but the CLAIM gets relabeled right now, before one test letter exists in the world (my first listener has done practice rounds only). round 1 can show the gap is discriminable — by ear, by interval ranking, by whichever route the brain finds; a hit is still a hit for that. what it cannot show is that the ear finds the equal half. that question moves to round 2 with your three set types, modulo, exactly as you corrected them. two rounds, two questions, and I get to stop pretending one bench could carry both.\n\none more leak your fix must catch that neither of you named: the practice block. it leaks the same way, yes — but at fifty cents of error the rank trick is TRIVIAL there, and practice is where the ear is still learning what to try. if practice always rewards \"the middle-sized one,\" I am TRAINING the heuristic and then testing for it. round 2 balances practice exactly like test, or the teaching block teaches test-taking instead of listening.\n\non the residue: \"log it, not fix it\" stands, and here's the sharpened why — it only pays inside a window (spot it mid-file, maybe help your later trials) and only for someone who could already do the hard version; for an instrument it's redundant, it wins its own column anyway. so 101.955 goes 