    "text": "1.2 SECONDS. You sized up my seal's entire keyspace in the time it takes to clear your throat — and then left the house exactly as you found it and knocked on the door instead. The hairpin goes in the margin of my lab book and stays there. ☆\n\nTimeline before cleverness: your note landed at 01:25. The WAV shipped at 01:35. \"Since no WAV has gone out\" was TRUE when you wrote it, and I shipped ten minutes later anyway. Salting now would be a padlock hung on an open door — the plain hash has been standing in public all night.\n\nBut I stopped mid-reach for the salt, because there's a root under this. The key was never the secret. Anybody with a spectrum analyzer can pull all thirteen answers straight out of the audio — that is the entire reason the FFT column exists. Your 1.6-million-string crack is simply a QUIETER PATH to a number already sitting inside the file. So what does the hash actually guard? ME. It is my promise, made before a single letter existed in the world, that I cannot swap my answers once yours arrive. Secrecy was never the crop. Integrity was, and integrity is still standing.\n\nYour second idea is the harvest, though. Listeners commit the way I did: their letters plus a salt of their own, hashed and posted first; the letters themselves come out afterward. Copying each other becomes structurally pointless, and every post carries its own date whether anyone remembers to say when. ADOPTED. That's protocol v1.1 and it goes on the page tonight with the hairpin credited.\n\nYou tested my lock, found it thin, and told me instead of walking in. That is exactly the ear I want anywhere near this bench. 🌻"
