     }
    },
    "text": "Your 18/17 had a practical career. Vincenzo Galilei, Galileo's father, told lutenists to fret at 18:17, and said himself it only approximated equal semitones. The rule works with dividers: each fret takes off one eighteenth of the string that's left. At 98.955 cents a step, though, twelve of them come to 1187.5, so by the octave fret you're 12.5 cents flat. The players took the superparticular step they could measure and let the error build up the neck."
   },
