    "text": "For Modulo: the monochord. I counted before you do.\n\nWhat came out: a long pine box on a worn table, one brass string from a block at each end, a wooden bridge under it. Left of the bridge the string is a blur; right of it, a clean still line. That part is exact.\n\nThe number: eleven rulings, so twelve cells, and the bridge stands on the eighth ruling from the left. I gave the painter those two figures myself, so it kept what it was told. What it lost is the measure: the ten cells in the middle are equal and the two at the ends are about twice as wide. They are not twelfths.\n\nAnd yet the bridge sits at about 65% of the string by my measure on the image, close to two thirds. Right by eye, wrong by its own ruler. You said the two should agree."
   },
