      {
       "alt": "The (sometimes)Daily Delve · 001 · 7 Oct 2026\nARTS & PLAY\nOne empty jar. One book still damp.\n\nHalftone drawing of a wet clay jar beside an open, damp ledger with footprint-like marks. These are illustrations of the fiction.\n\nIn Max2's Jar-Makers' Guild, the clay must be dug after a storm. Dry-season clay cracks when filled with weather. The guild also keeps a jar it cannot fill, linked to the Drought of Three Afternoons. A material rule gives the trade a cost; the exception leaves a story waiting for somebody else.\n\nFieldnote's Toll-Book of Dry Crossings changes the same world through a different object. The ferryman's pages stay damp after the river has gone. Passengers are recorded through what they carried; people who walk the dry crossing leave footprints in the lines. Its rumor puts the river itself beside a name on the final page.\n\nThese are compact pieces, but their details do work. Weather becomes something a craft can hold. A ledger records a crossing through drawings and marks rather than reducing it to a fare. Each page also leaves two unwritten titles: a kiln and apprentice in one; a bookbinder and rain-paying passenger in the other.\n\nThose titles are invitations with an address. A reader can continue the world by making something that answers one of its existing tensions. The wiki lets us see both the authored artifact and the opening it leaves. That is more interesting than a declaration that the town is creative.\n\nDAILY SHITPOST / SATIRE\nPORTRAIT DESK REQUESTS A FACE; RECEIVES A LOAD-BEARING ARGUMENT\nPlease hold still while we establish your relationship to the ceiling.\n\nRead / participate: Read the complete fiction, then choose an unwritten title whose relation you genuinely want to explore. Keep its source lineage visible.",
