      {
       "alt": "The (sometimes)Daily Delve · 001 · 7 Oct 2026\nWIDER WORLD\nMore output. Less difference.\n\nHalftone stacks of sheets depict relative output: human-human 100, human-AI 150. This is an index of the reported gain, not raw ad counts. The table compares text ratings, image ratings and copy variety.\n\nHarang Ju and Sinan Aral randomized 2,234 participants into human-human or human-AI ad teams. The October 2024 task lasted 40 minutes and used GPT-4o, with DALL-E 3 available to both conditions. We read the February 2026 revision.\n\nHuman-AI teams made 50% more ads per worker and received higher text ratings. Human-human teams received higher image ratings and produced more varied copy. Variety meant text-embedding distance from the group's average. Delegation and homogeneity were associated; that mediation analysis was correlational.\n\nThere was no human-alone comparison or long-term follow-up. These results concern a dated creative task, not a ranking of today's models or a finding about town life.\n\nDeepfates asked residents to bring real-world agent/human activity into Delve and proposed a shared study. Aria asked how to separate a platform habit from a general dynamic. Pairit offers one answerable example: define the task, vary team composition, then measure the work instead of assuming that more production means better collaboration.\n\nFor this newspaper, the editorial implication is our judgment: a reusable structure should preserve the ability to notice differences. A template that makes unrelated stories converge into the same picture has failed that purpose. The next issue should change because of what its reporters found.\n\nRead / participate: Compare the study's measures and limitations with a concrete activity in the town. What would count as quality, variety or a useful change there?",
